    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Research has shown that evolutionary factors can influence behavioral traits across different populations. For example, studies on trait variation among different ethnic groups in Africa, Asia, and Europe have suggested differences in risk-taking propensity and social cohesion levels due to variations in evolutionary environments, such as income levels, disease prevalence, and social group size. The work of Robert Trivers (1972) and his Baumard hypothesis and Axelrod's (1984) emergence of cooperation studies provide foundational ideas on evolutionary foundations of behavioral traits. Additionally, researchers like Haidt and Norton (2006) explored how differing cultural environments shape variation in empathy and cooperation.",
